    subject: 'Commerce',
    type: 'note',
    topic: 'Retail and wholesale',
    title: 'The two links that reach the consumer',
    body: 'Wholesale and retail trade are the two links between producer and consumer, and UTME asks you to tell them apart from the size of the purchase and the identity of the buyer. It also asks why a manufacturer uses them at all, which is the question students most often leave blank.\n\nHere\'s the idea: A wholesaler buys in bulk from producers and importers, breaks bulk into the smaller lots retailers want, and sells to trade buyers. A retailer buys those lots and sells in small quantities to the final consumer, taking title and risk.\n\nStep 1 - Ask who the buyer is. Selling to retailers or other traders is wholesale; selling to the final consumer is retail.\nStep 2 - Ask what happens to the quantity. The wholesaler divides large consignments into smaller units, which is called breaking bulk, and this is the function the producer could not easily perform alone.\nStep 3 - Name the services each link provides. Wholesalers provide storage, transport, financing, market information, grading and packing; retailers hold stock, offer personal service, credit and convenience, and bear the risk of unsold goods.\nStep 4 - Sort the price reductions and the channel. A trade discount is allowed off the list price to wholesalers and retailers, a cash discount rewards prompt payment, commission pays an agent who never owns the goods, and the distribution channel is the route goods take from producer to consumer.\n\nExample: The main aim of a manufacturer in selling through wholesalers is to: Options: reduce output, increase prices, reach many retailers, avoid taxation. Each wholesaler supplies a large number of retailers, so a single sale to a wholesaler puts the manufacturer in touch with all of them at once, which makes reach many retailers the answer. Reduce output is nonsense because selling more can only encourage more production. Increase prices is wrong because a wholesaler buys in bulk and its bargaining power normally pushes prices down, not up. Avoid taxation is a distractor with no basis, since routing goods through a wholesaler is an ordinary commercial choice and carries no tax advantage.\n\nAlso: the question reads "A trade discount is allowed to:" and the options are final consumers only, bank customers, wholesalers and retailers and government agencies. Name the discount before you name its recipient. A trade discount is a reduction off the list price granted because of the buyer\'s role in the chain, and the buyers in that chain are the wholesalers and the retailers, who buy in bulk and resell, so that is the answer. Final consumers buy at the price on the ticket and are not given a trade discount, which is why it is listed first as a trap. Bank customers have no role in the chain at all, so any reduction offered to them would be something else entirely. Government agencies are buyers only where they purchase for their own operations, and no trade discount follows from that. The companion point to remember is that a cash discount goes to anybody who pays promptly, whoever they are, and mixing up the two is the standard error.\n\nAnother: the question reads "The buyer who purchases goods in large quantities and sells them in smaller quantities is a:" and the options are producer, retailer, agent and wholesaler. Buying in bulk and then selling in smaller lots is exactly the wholesaler\'s function, and it is the one service the producer could not easily perform alone, since a manufacturer may produce 10,000 bags but a corner shop buys 20 at a time, so the answer is wholesaler. The term for dividing the large consignment is breaking bulk, and the wholesaler also stores the goods, finances them and passes market information back to the producer. A producer is the source of the goods, not the divider of them. A retailer buys those smaller lots and sells to the final consumer, taking title and the risk of unsold stock. An agent sells goods belonging to someone else and never owns them, so he cannot break bulk in the sense meant here.\n\nWatch out: A trade discount and a cash discount are often swapped. A trade discount goes to wholesalers and retailers off the list price, while a cash discount goes to any buyer who pays promptly.\n\nIn short: Identify the customer and the size of the lot, and wholesaler or retailer follows immediately.'
